  "content": {
    "id": "p14-23d-q7-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "7",
    "prompt": "Let $m$ and $n$ be positive integers. Show that $x^n-1$ is divisible by $x^m-1$ if and only if $n$ is a multiple of $m$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$x^m-1$ divides $x^n-1$ if and only if $m\\mid n$.\n\nWriting $n=qm+r$, where $0\\le r<m$, gives remainder $x^r-1$, which is zero exactly when $r=0$.",
      "worked": "Write $n=qm+r$, where $q\\ge0$ and $0\\le r<m$. For $q\\ge1$, let\n$$S(x)=x^r\\sum_{j=0}^{q-1}x^{jm}.$$\nFor $q=0$, let $S(x)=0$. Cancelling successive terms gives\n$$\\begin{align*}(x^m-1)S(x)&=x^n-x^r,\\\\x^n-1&=(x^m-1)S(x)+(x^r-1).\\end{align*}$$\nIf $r>0$, the remainder $x^r-1$ is nonzero and has degree $r<m$. If $r=0$, the remainder is the zero polynomial. Therefore, $x^n-1$ is divisible by $x^m-1$ exactly when $r=0$, which is exactly when $n$ is a multiple of $m$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 65
  },
